# Ship the extension, not the repository.
#
# An allow-list rather than a deny-list, because the deny-list this
# replaced shipped every workflow, every tools/ script,
# .pre-commit-config.yaml, .github/actionlint.yaml, AGENTS.md,
# ARCHITECTURE.md, PUSH-AUDIT.md, REVIEWS.md, RELEASE-SETUP.md,
# renovate.json and biome.json to every user: 24 of the 27 packaged
# files were repository infrastructure rather than the extension.
# Phase 7 is the phase that makes the .vsix public, so it is the phase
# that has to decide what is in it.
#
# The consequence to know about: a new file has to be named here to
# reach a user. That is the point -- the failure mode of a deny-list is
# silent, and this one's was.
**

!package.json
!README.md
!LICENSE
!out/src/**/*.js

# Note the allow rule ends in *.js, not out/src/**. A trailing
# "**/*.map" deny does NOT override an earlier "!" allow in vsce's
# matcher -- it was tried, and the maps shipped anyway. Naming only the
# .js is what actually keeps them out, and a map is useless here
# regardless, since the .ts sources it points at are not in the package.
#
# The "**/" is equally load bearing, and was missing until a push audit
# measured what the rule actually selects. A single-segment "*" does not
# cross a directory separator, so "!out/src/*.js" allowed
# out/src/diff.js and excluded out/src/anything/else.js. The first
# module added under a subdirectory of src/ would have compiled, tested
# and packaged without complaint, and then failed at require() time on
# every user's machine and nowhere else. tools/check-package-contents.sh
# packages a nested probe module and fails if it does not ship, so this
# cannot quietly come back.
